Source: PHP Capstone project — tenant-concierge-debug-log.md. Discovered during build and testing of PHP_Tenant_Concierge. Verified Spring ‘26.


1. @InvocableMethod Does Not Accept Inner Classes Without @InvocableVariable Fields
Symptom: Deploy fails with:

“InvocableMethod methods do not support parameter type of List&lt;ClassName.InnerClassName&gt;”

Root cause: Salesforce’s @InvocableMethod rejects inner class types as parameters unless the inner class has at least one field annotated with @InvocableVariable. Empty inner classes are explicitly rejected.
Pattern that fails:
public class MyAction {
    public class Input {} // empty — no @InvocableVariable fields

    @InvocableMethod
    public static List&lt;String&gt; execute(List&lt;Input&gt; inputs) { ... }
}
Resolution: Use a primitive type or a List&lt;String&gt; when no input parameters are actually needed:
@InvocableMethod
public static List&lt;String&gt; execute(List&lt;String&gt; inputs) {
    // inputs can be ignored if no parameters are required
}
Or add at least one @InvocableVariable field to the inner class if you need a typed parameter:
public class Input {
    @InvocableVariable public String someField;
}

2. &lt;versionIdentifier&gt; in Prompt Template Metadata Must Be Removed
Symptom: Prompt Template deployment fails with:

“The prompt template version identifier ‘&lt;your-value&gt;’ is invalid.”

Root cause: The &lt;versionIdentifier&gt; element in &lt;TemplateName&gt;.genAiPromptTemplate-meta.xml must be a Salesforce-generated hash — not a human-readable string like MyTemplate_v1. Salesforce auto-generates this value on first deploy; if the element is present with any non-hash value, the deploy fails.
Resolution: Remove the &lt;versionIdentifier&gt; tag entirely from the XML file before deploying:
&lt;!-- Remove this line entirely: --&gt;
&lt;!-- &lt;versionIdentifier&gt;MyTemplate_v1&lt;/versionIdentifier&gt; --&gt;
Salesforce will auto-generate a valid identifier on deploy. On subsequent retrieves, the element will be present with the generated hash — do not replace it with a human-readable string.

3. AccessLevel.USER_MODE on DML Fails FLS in Test Context for Agent Actions
Symptom: Apex test fails with:

“System.DmlException: Operation failed due to fields being inaccessible on &lt;ObjectName&gt;__c”

Root cause: Database.insert(record, AccessLevel.USER_MODE) enforces Field-Level Security (FLS). In the test context running as a user with limited permissions (e.g. an agent running user or test user without full object access), FLS checks fail on custom object fields.
Context: This pattern appears in agent action classes that write OTP session records, case records, or other transactional objects. The agent running user is intentionally low-privilege (least-privilege model), so USER_MODE will fail for these objects.
Resolution: Use AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE for DML operations in agent action classes, with security enforced at the agent action level instead:
// Instead of:
Database.insert(session, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);

// Use:
Database.insert(session, AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE);
Defense-in-depth justification: Security for agent actions is enforced by:

The agent’s topic guard (e.g. VerifyActiveTenant gate runs before any DML action)
The action’s own input validation (e.g. ContactId must match the authenticated session user)
The agent running user’s Permission Set (restricts which actions are callable)

Field-level security at the DML layer adds friction without adding meaningful security when these three layers are already in place.

4. OTP Email: setTargetObjectId Alone Does Not Deliver in Org Farm Environments
Symptom: Agent shows “A 6-digit code has been sent to r****@gmail.com” but no email arrives in the inbox. The OTP_Session__c record is created and OrgLimits confirms the SingleEmail API was called. Email deliverability is set to “All email.”
Root cause: Messaging.SingleEmailMessage.setTargetObjectId(contactId) routes email through Salesforce’s Contact delivery pipeline. In org farm / pc-rnd environments, this path is intercepted and email is not delivered to external inboxes.
Fix: Use both setTargetObjectId AND setToAddresses together:
Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
mail.setTargetObjectId(contactId);           // associates email as Activity on Person Account
mail.setToAddresses(new String[]{emailAddr}); // direct delivery to email string, bypasses Contact pipeline
mail.setSaveAsActivity(true);                 // creates EmailMessage record for audit trail
mail.setSubject(&#039;Your one-time code&#039;);
mail.setPlainTextBody(&#039;Your code: &#039; + otpCode);
Messaging.sendEmail(new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage[]{ mail });
Effect of each parameter:

setTargetObjectId(contactId) → associates the email as an Activity on the Person Account record (visible in Activity timeline — useful for audit)
setToAddresses([email]) → sends directly to the email string, bypassing the Contact delivery pipeline
setSaveAsActivity(true) → creates an EmailMessage record on the Contact/Person Account

Result: Email is visible in the Person Account’s Activity timeline AND delivered to the external inbox in org farm environments.

5. Prompt Template Deployment: &lt;templateType&gt; Must Match Usage Context
Observed issue (related): Prompt Templates deployed for agent grounding must have &lt;templateType&gt; set correctly. Templates targeting agent actions require FieldGeneration or SalesEmail types depending on usage context. Using the wrong type causes silent failure at runtime (template is found but not invoked).
Guidance: Check the Prompt Builder UI for the template type before writing metadata. Let Salesforce generate the metadata via Prompt Builder first, then retrieve and commit — do not hand-author genAiPromptTemplate-meta.xml from scratch.

Quick Reference: Common Deploy Failures



































SymptomLikely CauseFix”InvocableMethod does not support parameter type”Empty inner class as parameterUse List&lt;String&gt; or add @InvocableVariable to inner class”The prompt template version identifier is invalid”&lt;versionIdentifier&gt; tag present with non-hash valueRemove the &lt;versionIdentifier&gt; tag entirelyTest DmlException on custom object fieldsAccessLevel.USER_MODE + limited test userUse AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE, enforce security at action levelOTP email sent but not receivedsetTargetObjectId onlyAdd setToAddresses + setSaveAsActivity(true)Agent preview crashes with no trace@utils.escalate in scriptRemove @utils.escalate, use static handoff message — see agentforce-builder-preview-gotchas.md

Agentforce/13-Licensing--and--Limits/age.content:
Source: PHP Capstone project — ops-copilot-metering-cost-notes.md. Validated during Ops Copilot weekly summary design.


Metered vs. Unmetered Actions























TypeDefinitionExamplesCredit ImpactMeteredAny action that invokes LLM generationPrompt Template execution, agent reasoning step, generative summaryConsumes Flex Credits / AI usage credits per callUnmeteredDeterministic platform actions that do not invoke LLM generationApex callout, Flow execution, SOQL query, data retrievalNo credit consumption
Key rule: If a step produces generated text (summary, email draft, narrative), it is metered. If a step retrieves, transforms, or routes data deterministically, it is unmetered.

Scheduled Jobs and Flex Credit Implications
A scheduled Agentforce workflow that generates content on each run consumes one metered conversation per execution.
Example: A scheduled Flow that runs weekly for 50 property managers, where each run invokes a Prompt Template to generate a written summary:

50 managers × 52 weeks = 2,600 metered conversations per year from one scheduled job alone.

Example (unmetered): A scheduled Apex job that aggregates occupancy, late payments, and open cases into a Weekly_Summary__c record. No LLM call. Zero Flex Credits consumed.

Low-Cost Design Pattern: Pre-Aggregate + Narrate On Demand
The recommended pattern for any scheduled or recurring data summary:
Step 1 [Unmetered] — Scheduled Apex or Flow
        Aggregate raw data → write to a custom summary object
        (e.g. Weekly_Property_Summary__c)
        Runs on schedule. No LLM call. No credits consumed.

Step 2 [Metered, on demand] — Agent Action
        Manager asks the agent: &quot;Show me this week&#039;s summary&quot;
        Agent reads the pre-computed record → invokes Prompt Template
        to narrate the numbers in plain English.
        One metered call, triggered by human request.

Savings: For N property managers receiving weekly summaries, this pattern saves N − 1 metered conversations per week compared to generating a summary for every manager on schedule.
Additional benefit: The on-demand narrative is always fresh from the manager’s perspective (they get it when they ask), while the underlying data is pre-aggregated and fast to retrieve.

Implementation Notes
// Scheduled Apex: aggregates data, writes summary record (unmetered)
public class BuildWeeklyOpsSummaryScheduled implements Schedulable {
    public void execute(SchedulableContext ctx) {
        // Query Apartment__c, Lease_Payment__c, Case for each manager
        // Write/update Weekly_Property_Summary__c records
        // No LLM call here
    }
}

// Agent action: reads pre-computed record, calls Prompt Template (metered, on demand)
// Prompt Template receives:
//   {!summaryRecord.Occupancy_Rate__c}
//   {!summaryRecord.Late_Payments__c}
//   {!summaryRecord.Open_Cases__c}
// LLM narrates: &quot;This week, occupancy is at 94%...&quot;

Monitoring Credit Consumption

Einstein Usage &amp; Limits (Setup) — shows conversation credit and Flex Credit consumption in real time.
Set a conversation cap on dev/sandbox channels to prevent runaway credit usage during build and testing.
Production agents should have a monitoring alert on Flex Credit burn rate.


Quick Reference: Design Decisions






























ScenarioRecommended ApproachReasonWeekly property summary for N managersScheduled Apex aggregation → on-demand LLM narrationSaves N−1 metered calls per weekReal-time tenant history lookupApex query + return structured data; LLM narrates on requestApex query is unmetered; LLM call is one-time per requestRenewal email draftApex computes branch (warm/strict/decline) → Prompt Template narratesApex branching is unmetered; only the email generation is meteredRepeated FAQ answersData Library RAG retrievalRAG retrieval is metered but typically cheaper than custom generation; cache with KM Articles for frequent queries

CTA-Notes/Data-Management/LDV/bulk-api-a.content:
Sample Build Scenario — “Meridian Retail”
Used throughout this note to make each feature concrete. Meridian Retail is migrating off a legacy on-prem ERP into Salesforce and has six distinct data-movement needs, each mapped to a different feature below:















































#NeedVolumeFeature used1One-time historical load of closed Orders8M recordsBulk API 2.0 ingest2Nightly incremental extract of Accounts/Contacts to a BI warehouse~3M records/nightBulk API 2.0 query3Legacy invoicing middleware writing ledger-ordered Invoice records500k records, strict orderBulk API 1.0 ingest (serial mode)4One-off compliance audit extraction of historical Cases12M recordsBulk API 1.0 query + PK chunking5”My Open Orders” panel in a Lightning component&lt; 2,000 records/callREST API query + nextRecordsUrl cursor6Nightly Apex job recalculating loyalty tier from lifetime spend4M ContactsApex Database.Cursor

Bulk API 1.0 vs 2.0 Comparison
(Bulk API 2.0 and Bulk API Developer Guide — Introduction)




























































FeatureBulk API 1.0Bulk API 2.0ProtocolSOAP-based XMLREST + CSVBatch managementManual — you create and monitor batchesAutomatic — platform manages batchingMax records per job50M150M records/24h per orgBatch sizeUp to 10k records per batchManaged automaticallyConcurrency modesParallel or Serial (explicit choice)Parallel onlyPK chunkingManual opt-in via header (query jobs)Commonly described as automatic for query jobs, but this could not be independently re-confirmed on the current Bulk API 2.0 Query page as of 2026-09-30 — treat as likely, not confirmedSupported operationsinsert, update, upsert, delete, hardDelete, queryinsert, update, upsert, delete, hardDelete, queryResults retrievalPer-batch result filesSingle stream, paginatedEase of useMore complex; need to manage batch lifecycleSimpler; job + upload data + pollIdeal forLegacy integrations; serial mode for order-dependencyNew integrations; data migrations



                  Default choice 



Use Bulk API 2.0 for all new work. 1.0 is only needed when you require serial concurrency mode or are integrating with a legacy system that already uses it.





                  Hard delete 



hardDelete requires the “Bulk API Hard Delete” permission. Use with caution — records are not recoverable from the recycle bin.





                  Build it: Meridian Retail — need #3 (ledger-ordered Invoices) 



Meridian’s legacy invoicing middleware must write Invoice__c records in strict chronological order because downstream ledger reconciliation depends on insert order. This is exactly the case the comparison table calls out: Bulk API 1.0, Serial concurrency mode. In the job request:
// POST to the Bulk API 1.0 job creation endpoint
{
  &quot;operation&quot;: &quot;insert&quot;,
  &quot;object&quot;: &quot;Invoice__c&quot;,
  &quot;contentType&quot;: &quot;CSV&quot;,
  &quot;concurrencyMode&quot;: &quot;Serial&quot;
}
Serial mode processes batches one at a time in submission order — slower, but guarantees no two Invoice__c batches are ever committed out of order. Bulk API 2.0 doesn’t offer this mode, which is why the legacy system is deliberately kept on 1.0 rather than migrated.



Bulk API 2.0 — Job Lifecycle
(Bulk API 2.0 Ingest · Get Job Information · Upload Job Data)
Create Job → Upload CSV batches → Close Job → Poll for completion → Retrieve results


POST /jobs/ingest — create job, specify object and operation
PUT /jobs/ingest/{jobId}/batches — upload CSV (repeat for large files)
PATCH /jobs/ingest/{jobId} with state: UploadComplete
GET /jobs/ingest/{jobId} — poll until state: JobComplete
GET /jobs/ingest/{jobId}/successfulResults and failedResults




                  Build it: Meridian Retail — need #1 (8M historical Orders) 



The one-time historical Order migration is the textbook Bulk API 2.0 ingest case: a single, huge, one-shot load with no ordering requirement.
1. POST /services/data/v62.0/jobs/ingest
   { &quot;object&quot;: &quot;Order&quot;, &quot;operation&quot;: &quot;insert&quot;, &quot;lineEnding&quot;: &quot;LF&quot; }
   → returns jobId + contentUrl

2. PUT {contentUrl}   (repeat per CSV chunk, e.g. 10 files of ~800k rows each)
   Content-Type: text/csv
   OrderNumber,AccountId,EffectiveDate,Status
   LEG-000123,001xx0000...,2019-03-01,Activated
   ...

3. PATCH /services/data/v62.0/jobs/ingest/{jobId}
   { &quot;state&quot;: &quot;UploadComplete&quot; }

4. GET /services/data/v62.0/jobs/ingest/{jobId}   (poll every 30-60s until JobComplete)

5. GET /services/data/v62.0/jobs/ingest/{jobId}/failedResults
   → feed rejected rows into a remediation CSV for a second pass

At 8M records against the 150M-records/24h org cap, this single job consumes a modest slice of the daily allocation — worth flagging in a design review if Meridian also runs nightly loads (need #2) on the same day.



Bulk Query (1.0 vs 2.0)



































Bulk API 1.0 QueryBulk API 2.0 QueryDocBulk API QueryBulk API 2.0 QueryPK chunkingManual opt-in via Sforce-Enable-PKChunking headerLikely automatic — not independently confirmed, see caution aboveResult formatXML result files per batchCSV, paginatedqueryAll supportYes (includes soft-deleted records)YesUse caseLegacy ETL toolsModern data extraction, migrations



                  Note



For reporting/extraction at scale, prefer Bulk API 2.0 Query + cursor pagination over REST API queryMore. The REST API query cursor has a short inactivity expiry and is not suited for very large result sets.





                  Build it: Meridian Retail — need #2 (nightly 3M-record BI extract) 



A scheduled job (outside Salesforce — e.g. an integration platform) runs nightly:
1. POST /services/data/v62.0/jobs/query
   { &quot;operation&quot;: &quot;query&quot;, &quot;query&quot;: &quot;SELECT Id, Name, LastModifiedDate FROM Account WHERE LastModifiedDate = TODAY&quot; }

2. GET /services/data/v62.0/jobs/query/{jobId}   (poll until JobComplete)

3. GET /services/data/v62.0/jobs/query/{jobId}/results
   → CSV stream, paginated via the `Sforce-Locator` response header

Using LastModifiedDate = TODAY (delta only, not a full extract) keeps each night’s job well under the 3M full-table size and avoids re-shipping unchanged rows to the warehouse.



Salesforce Cursors
There are three distinct “cursor” concepts in Salesforce — easy to conflate.
1. REST API Query Cursor
(Query Resource)

Created implicitly when a SOQL query result exceeds the batch size (default, and max, 2,000 records per synchronous response — confirmed on the current Query resource page)
Returns nextRecordsUrl in the response; call it repeatedly to page through results
This note previously stated a hard “10-minute inactivity expiry” and a “max 10 cursors per user session” — neither figure could be re-confirmed on the current Query resource page as of 2026-09-30. Treat both as unverified rather than exam-quotable facts until a live source is found.
Not suitable for LDV extraction — use Bulk API 2.0 instead

// Response excerpt
{
  &quot;totalSize&quot;: 50000,
  &quot;done&quot;: false,
  &quot;nextRecordsUrl&quot;: &quot;/services/data/v62.0/query/01gxx000000...&quot;
}



                  Build it: Meridian Retail — need #5 (&quot;My Open Orders&quot; panel) 



A Lightning web component calls Apex, which runs:
String q = &#039;SELECT Id, OrderNumber, Status, TotalAmount FROM Order &#039; +
           &#039;WHERE OwnerId = :UserInfo.getUserId() AND Status != \&#039;Activated\&#039; &#039; +
           &#039;ORDER BY EffectiveDate DESC&#039;;
List&lt;Order&gt; orders = Database.query(q); // &lt; 2,000 rows for a single rep — no pagination needed
Because a single rep’s open-order count stays well under 2,000, this never triggers nextRecordsUrl pagination in practice — it’s the “small, real-time” end of the decision guide below. If Meridian later adds a “team open orders” rollup that could exceed 2,000 rows, that’s the trigger to move to Bulk API 2.0 query rather than chase REST pagination.


2. Apex Database Cursor (Summer ‘24+)
(Database.Cursor class reference)
A native Apex API for scrollable, stateful SOQL iteration — designed specifically for LDV processing without chaining Batch Apex jobs.
Key facts (re-verified 2026-09-30):

Created with Database.getCursor(query) or Database.getCursorWithBinds(query, bindMap, accessLevel)
Scrollable: fetch(position, count) — up to 2,000 records per call
Max 50 million rows per cursor, sync or async
Max 100 Cursor.fetch() calls per transaction
Calling fetch() counts against the SOQL query limit, and rows fetched count against the SOQL row limit — this is a real governor-limit cost, not a free ride
This note previously stated “cursor valid for 24 hours,” “max 5 open cursors per org,” and “does not consume Batch Apex concurrency slots.” None of these three could be re-confirmed on the current class reference page as of 2026-09-30 — the page instead confirms fetch() calls DO count against ordinary SOQL limits. Treat the three unconfirmed figures as legacy/unverified, not settled facts.

Typical use pattern:
Database.Cursor cursor = Database.getCursor(&#039;SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE ...&#039;);
Integer totalSize = cursor.getNumRecords();

while (cursor.getNumRecords() &gt; 0) {
    List&lt;Account&gt; batch = (List&lt;Account&gt;) cursor.fetch(Account.class, 2000);
    // process batch
}
cursor.close();



                  When to use Database Cursor vs Batch Apex 



Use Database Cursor when you need stateful, scrollable iteration in a single Apex context (e.g. a Queueable chain) and don’t need parallel processing. Use Batch Apex when you need parallel execution or the volume exceeds what a single cursor lifecycle can handle.





                  Warning



Database Cursor is a relatively new feature (Summer ‘24). Confirm API version support in the target org before recommending it in a CTA scenario, and remember fetch() calls consume ordinary SOQL governor limits — a naive loop can still hit LimitException.





                  Build it: Meridian Retail — need #6 (nightly loyalty-tier recalc, 4M Contacts) 



public class LoyaltyTierRecalcQueueable implements Queueable {
    Integer position;
    public LoyaltyTierRecalcQueueable(Integer startPosition) { this.position = startPosition; }

    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        Database.Cursor cursor = Database.getCursor(
            &#039;SELECT Id, Lifetime_Spend__c, Loyalty_Tier__c FROM Contact WHERE Lifetime_Spend__c &gt; 0&#039;
        );
        List&lt;Contact&gt; batch = (List&lt;Contact&gt;) cursor.fetch(position, 2000);
        List&lt;Contact&gt; toUpdate = new List&lt;Contact&gt;();
        for (Contact c : batch) {
            String newTier = TierCalculator.tierFor(c.Lifetime_Spend__c);
            if (newTier != c.Loyalty_Tier__c) {
                c.Loyalty_Tier__c = newTier;
                toUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) update toUpdate;
        cursor.close();

        if (!batch.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; !Test.isRunningTest()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new LoyaltyTierRecalcQueueable(position + 2000));
        }
    }
}
At 4M Contacts ÷ 2,000/fetch = 2,000 fetch calls total — well over the 100 fetch() calls per transaction limit, so this must be chained across multiple Queueable transactions (as shown, re-enqueuing every batch) rather than looped in one execution. This is exactly the kind of governor-limit interaction the newly-confirmed 100-calls-per-transaction limit changes about how you’d design this job.


3. Bulk API Cursor (PK Chunking)
(Enable PK Chunking)

Splits a large query into sub-queries by primary key range (chunks)
Enabled via Sforce-Enable-PKChunking: true header
Default chunk size: 100,000; max: 250,000 (confirmed on the current guide page)
Each chunk is processed as a separate batch that counts toward the daily batch limit
Whether Bulk API 2.0 query jobs still need this header manually, or chunk automatically, could not be confirmed — see the caution in the comparison table above




                  Build it: Meridian Retail — need #4 (12M-record compliance audit of historical Cases) 



The audit tool is a legacy Bulk API 1.0 client, so PK chunking must be opted in manually:
POST /services/async/62.0/job
Header: Sforce-Enable-PKChunking: chunkSize=200000

&lt;jobInfo&gt;
  &lt;operation&gt;query&lt;/operation&gt;
  &lt;object&gt;Case&lt;/object&gt;
  &lt;contentType&gt;CSV&lt;/contentType&gt;
&lt;/jobInfo&gt;

12M records ÷ 200,000/chunk = 60 chunks → 60 auto-created batches, each independently retryable if one chunk times out — far more resilient than one 12M-row batch, and it avoids the query-level timeout that a single unchunked query against 12M Cases would likely hit.



Decision Guide: Which Approach to Use?


















































ScenarioRecommended approachMeridian Retail needOne-time data migration (millions of records)Bulk API 2.0 ingest#1 — historical OrdersScheduled nightly extract to data warehouseBulk API 2.0 query#2 — Account/Contact BI extractLegacy ETL requiring serial orderingBulk API 1.0 with serial concurrency#3 — ledger-ordered InvoicesVery large one-off query needing resilient chunkingBulk API 1.0/2.0 query + PK chunking#4 — Case compliance auditReal-time integration (&lt; 2k records/call)REST API (SOQL + nextRecordsUrl)#5 — Open Orders panelApex processing of large object set in-org, no parallelism neededDatabase Cursor (Queueable)#6 — loyalty tier recalcParallel large-volume Apex processingBatch Apex—Historical/soft-deleted record extractionBulk API 2.0 queryAll—

